## 4. Decision maintenance rules

1. A decision must identify the behavior or invariant it controls.
2. A decision must include its rationale and affected consequences.
3. Reversing an accepted decision creates a new entry and marks the old entry
   superseded; history is not deleted.
4. Changes to accepted decisions must be reflected in every affected
   specification.
5. Implementation details may be chosen without an entry when they do not
   change observable behavior, integrity, or reproducibility.
6. When implementation reveals a product-level conflict, pause the change and
   record the conflict as an open decision rather than silently choosing.

---

## 5. Summary

The foundation is sufficiently defined to begin the first local-image country
reasoning implementation. The remaining open items concern future interfaces,
regional prioritization, player-facing language, hint behavior, and study
methodology rather than the integrity of the first analysis pipeline.
